---
import "../styles/paper.css";
import "katex/dist/katex.min.css";
import katex from "katex";

const k = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true });
const kd = (tex: string) =>
  katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true, displayMode: true });
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Two players claim cells of an n by n grid; the first to own four corners of a square wins. The first player wins exactly when n is at least 5, by a machine-checked strategy. Play against it."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="masthead">
      <h1>The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards</h1>
      <p class="byline">Sean Reid · August 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="abstract">
      <strong>Abstract.</strong> Two players alternately claim cells of an
      <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid; the first to hold all four corners
      of an axis-aligned square wins. We show the first player has a winning strategy
      precisely when <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />, and that all smaller
      boards are drawn under optimal play. The strategy is explicit, and the
      theorem is verified end to end in Lean 4.
    </p>

    <figure class="game">
      <sag-board n="5"></sag-board>
      <figcaption>
        Figure 1. Play the game: you are O, the bot opens as P1. Change n to
        explore; below 5 × 5 you can always survive.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The game</h2>
    <p>
      Played on the cells of an <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid, P1 (X)
      moving first. A winning set is any
      <span
        set:html={k("\\{(r,c),\\,(r,c{+}s),\\,(r{+}s,c),\\,(r{+}s,c{+}s)\\}")}
      /> with <span set:html={k("s \\ge 1")} />: the four corners of an
      axis-aligned square, from 2 × 2 blocks up. A full board with no completed
      square is a draw.
    </p>

    <div class="theorem">
      <span class="label">Theorem 1.</span> The first player has a winning strategy
      in the square achievement game on the
      <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board if and only if
      <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />.
    </div>

    <h2>History</h2>
    <p>
      Achievement games on grids go back to Harary's animal achievement games;
      this page will carry the full story, the structure of the winning
      strategy, and the proof outline. The section is being written alongside
      the paper.
    </p>

    <h2>Proof outline</h2>
    <p>
      The strategy is built from two reflexes and a finite table of forcing
      plans confined to a corner region, so that a single verified table covers
      every board size. A saturation argument reduces the general case to
      finitely many sizes, each checked exhaustively:
    </p>
    <p
      set:html={kd(
        "\\text{check}(T, N_0) = \\texttt{true} \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\forall n \\ge N_0,\\; \\text{P1 wins on } n \\times n.",
      )}
    />

    <h2>Formalization and downloads</h2>
    <p>
      The proof is formalized in Lean 4: the game semantics, an executable
      verifier with a machine-checked soundness theorem, and the strategy tables
      as certificates; the assembly for arbitrary board size is in progress.
      Both artifacts are built from this repository on every deploy:
      <a href="downloads/square-achievement-paper.pdf">the paper (PDF)</a> and
      <a href="downloads/square-achievement-formalization.tar.gz"
        >the Lean development (tar.gz)</a
      >.
    </p>

    <footer>
      Code MIT · text CC BY 4.0 · built from the square-achievement repository.
    </footer>

    <script>
      import "../game/board";
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
